window.PA_SEARCH = [{"k":"framework","t":"Customer & Market Discovery","s":"Skill area in the framework","u":"framework.html#discovery","x":"The practice of understanding real user needs, market dynamics, and problem validation before committing real resources to build something. It's what keeps a product grounded in reality instead of assumptions."},{"k":"course","t":"Customer & Market Discovery · Limited Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-novice&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Start With a Problem Statement · Lesson 2: Know Who You're Building For · Lesson 3: Do the Research · Lesson 4: Validate Before You Build · Lesson 5: The Discipline of Doing It First"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Start With a Problem Statement","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Limited Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-novice&lesson=1","x":"Monday, week three Ledgerline sells invoicing software to companies with small finance teams. Maya joined three weeks ago as its second product manager. Lauren Brooks: Customers keep telling sales that billing feels clunky. Can you get a redesign of the billing dashboard on next quarter's roadmap? Maya Chen: Yes. Before we scope it, can I spend this week…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Know Who You're Building For","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Limited Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-novice&lesson=2","x":"Three people, one purchase Maya asks Rosa who else would care if payment matching changed. Rosa introduces her to two colleagues. Tom Okafor: I sign the renewal. If this closes the month two days faster, I'll pay more for it. If it just looks better, I won't. Priya Shah: Anything that reads our bank feed needs single sign-on and an audit trail. Without…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Do the Research","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Limited Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-novice&lesson=3","x":"Two weeks, three kinds of evidence Dev Patel: Three customer calls is a good story. Is it enough to bet a quarter on? Maya Chen: Not yet. I'll spend two days reading what's already out there, then talk to five more customers of different sizes, then look at how everyone else handles this. Primary research tells you how people really work. Secondary research…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Validate Before You Build","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Limited Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-novice&lesson=4","x":"Three months, or two weeks? Dev Patel: A proper matching engine, with bank feeds, rules and part-payments, is about three months for two engineers. Maya Chen: Before we spend that, I want to know if people will actually change how they work. What if, for two weeks, five customers send us their bank export and we match it for them with a spreadsheet and some…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Discipline of Doing It First","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Limited Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-novice&lesson=5","x":"Six weeks later The first release suggests matches, including part-payments with the invoice number in the reference, and shows why it chose each one. Rosa approves them with one click. Rosa Alvarez: Month-end took me about an hour and a half. And I didn't chase anyone who'd already paid. Lauren Brooks: So we never built the redesign? Maya Chen: We built…"},{"k":"course","t":"Customer & Market Discovery · Moderate Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Feedback Isn't a Box to Check · Lesson 2: Collecting Feedback That's Actually Actionable · Lesson 3: Close the Loop · Lesson 4: Feedback vs. Market Signal · Lesson 5: Make It a Cycle, Not an Event"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Feedback Isn't a Box to Check","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Feedback is a critical ingredient for user-centered products, not a formality. But gathering it well means reaching the right stakeholders , and in most products that's more than the end user: there are the people who use it, the people who administer it, the people who pay for it, and sometimes the people who have to approve it. Three habits make feedback…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Collecting Feedback That's Actually Actionable","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-moderate&lesson=2","x":"Data alone doesn't tell the whole story; anecdotes alone give a skewed one. Good feedback collection blends the two. Surveys and polls quantify sentiment, but only if the questions are tailored to each stakeholder group and specific enough to act on. A vague question yields a vague answer. One-on-one interviews capture the \"why\" behind a behaviour. This is…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Close the Loop","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-moderate&lesson=3","x":"Gathering feedback isn't enough. If users don't see that their input went anywhere, they stop giving it. Closing the loop is how you keep the channel alive. Users give feedback Team decides + acts Ship or explain why not Users see it landed Three practices that close it: be transparent about decisions — including when feedback can't be acted on and why;…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Feedback vs. Market Signal","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-moderate&lesson=4","x":"Not all user feedback reflects a broader market need. Some of it is one person's individual pain point; some of it points at a trend showing up across the industry. A big part of doing discovery well is telling those apart. Two techniques: Cross-functional reviews. Sit down regularly with sales, marketing, and support to discuss which feedback lines up with…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Make It a Cycle, Not an Event","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-moderate&lesson=5","x":"The reason discovery slips to \"after the solution's already moving\" is that it's treated as a phase you pass through once. The fix is to make it a cycle that runs continuously alongside delivery."},{"k":"course","t":"Customer & Market Discovery · Lots of Experience","s":"6 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-experienced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Build Around the Workflow, Not the Vision · Lesson 2: Focus Is a Strategic Advantage · Lesson 3: Integration and Compliance Aren't Later-Stage Problems · Lesson 4: The Buyer Is Not Always the User · Lesson 5: Design Pilots and Implementation With Discipline · Lesson 6: Credibility Is Earned, Not Presented"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Build Around the Workflow, Not the Vision","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-experienced&lesson=1","x":"Healthcare professionals operate in highly structured environments: established processes, compliance demands, severe time constraints. A product that seems valuable in theory can create real friction in practice if it asks clinicians, administrators, or billing staff to change too much too quickly. Startups often mistake interest during interviews for true…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Focus Is a Strategic Advantage","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-experienced&lesson=2","x":"Healthcare startups often feel pressure to appear enterprise-ready from day one, building bloated products packed with features for every use case a prospect mentions. The result is usually complicated, expensive to maintain, and unclear in its core value. The strongest healthcare SaaS products begin by solving one painful problem for one clearly defined…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Integration and Compliance Aren't Later-Stage Problems","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-experienced&lesson=3","x":"Standalone healthcare software often struggles to gain traction. Providers and health systems already run on EHRs, billing tools, scheduling systems, and internal reporting. If a new product can't connect to that environment, the integration burden shifts to the customer, and that burden can become a deal breaker. Integration needs to be part of the product…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Buyer Is Not Always the User","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-experienced&lesson=4","x":"In healthcare organizations, the person who loves a product is often not the one who approves the budget, signs the contract, or clears procurement. A clinician may strongly support a tool that improves their workflow, but the final decision can depend on department leadership, finance, IT, legal, or compliance. Startups that build based only on user…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Design Pilots and Implementation With Discipline","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-experienced&lesson=5","x":"Many healthcare SaaS startups treat pilots as loosely defined trial periods rather than structured validation opportunities. Without clear success criteria, ownership, and timelines, pilots often drag on without producing a real decision, continued engagement gets mistaken for progress. Step through what a disciplined pilot actually requires."},{"k":"lesson","t":"Credibility Is Earned, Not Presented","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 6","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-experienced&lesson=6","x":"Some startups chase credibility signals instead of actual readiness, polished branding, broad feature sets, large vision statements, while neglecting the harder work of validating demand and delivering repeatable results. In healthcare, credibility does matter, but it's earned through trust, consistency, and evidence rather than presentation alone. The most…"},{"k":"course","t":"Customer & Market Discovery · Advanced Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-advanced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Empathy Beyond Feedback · Lesson 2: Empathy Maps and Living Personas · Lesson 3: Discovery Systems, Not Discovery Sprints · Lesson 4: Making Discovery the Org's Habit · Lesson 5: Responsible Discovery at Stakes"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Empathy Beyond Feedback","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Advanced Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-advanced&lesson=1","x":"Empathy in product work means seeing the world through your users' eyes. It goes past collecting feedback: it's immersing yourself in someone's context to understand their daily experience, their frustrations, and what they're actually trying to achieve. And \"the user\" is usually several people with conflicting realities. In a healthcare product, for…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Empathy Maps and Living Personas","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Advanced Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-advanced&lesson=2","x":"An empathy map organizes what you learn into four quadrants: what the user says , thinks , does , and feels . The gaps between quadrants are often the most revealing part — what someone says versus what they actually do, or what they do versus how they feel about it. SAYS THINKS DOES FEELS in interviews and support tickets but doesn't voice the actual…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Discovery Systems, Not Discovery Sprints","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Advanced Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-advanced&lesson=3","x":"At an advanced level, a user-centric product is never \"finished.\" The work shifts from running discovery projects to maintaining a continuous improvement loop that refines the product against evolving user needs, without anyone having to schedule it. Watch the KPIs Study wins + fails Ship small change Validate it Three things make the loop run on its own:…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Making Discovery the Org's Habit","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Advanced Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-advanced&lesson=4","x":"The advanced move is getting an entire organization to operate with user understanding by default, so discovery isn't one person's job title. Practical ways to build that culture:"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Responsible Discovery at Stakes","s":"Customer & Market Discovery · Advanced Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=discovery-advanced&lesson=5","x":"In high-stakes domains, discovery also has to de-risk . When a feature touches decisions, safety, money, or compliance, understanding the user isn't enough on its own — you validate carefully before it ships. Pilot in controlled environments. Introduce new capability incrementally, to a small representative group, so a wrong assumption fails small. Validate…"},{"k":"framework","t":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping","s":"Skill area in the framework","u":"framework.html#strategy","x":"Translating market understanding and business goals into a coherent product direction, and a roadmap that credibly connects today's work to that direction rather than just a list of features."},{"k":"course","t":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Limited Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-novice&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: A Roadmap Is Not a Feature List · Lesson 2: Strategic vs. Tactical · Lesson 3: Link Every Theme to a Business Objective · Lesson 4: Prioritizing the Themes · Lesson 5: Realistic, Time-Bound Milestones"},{"k":"lesson","t":"A Roadmap Is Not a Feature List","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Limited Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-novice&lesson=1","x":"A roadmap is a strategic communication tool . Its job is to bridge the gap between where the product is going (the vision) and what the team does next (execution). A prioritized pile of requests doesn't do that job, it tells you what's next, but not why, or where it all adds up to. The difference shows up in how you'd answer a simple question: \"Why is this…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Strategic vs. Tactical","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Limited Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-novice&lesson=2","x":"Roadmap work operates on two levels, and confusing them is where a lot of roadmaps go wrong. Themes / milestones Epics / features Tasks Strategic planning defines the high-level themes and milestones that support the vision, \"improve onboarding completion,\" \"reach enterprise readiness,\" \"cut time-to-value in half.\" That's what belongs on the roadmap.…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Link Every Theme to a Business Objective","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Limited Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-novice&lesson=3","x":"A roadmap that reflects strategy is one where each theme maps to a specific business goal, revenue growth, market expansion, retention, adoption. If you can't draw that line for a theme, it probably shouldn't be a theme. Theme Business objective Cut onboarding drop-off Ship SSO + audit log Refresh the settings screen Retention Enterprise expansion ? &#8212;…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Prioritizing the Themes","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Limited Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-novice&lesson=4","x":"Even a strategy-linked roadmap has more themes than capacity. Prioritizing them is a systematic process, not a vibe."},{"k":"lesson","t":"Realistic, Time-Bound Milestones","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Limited Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-novice&lesson=5","x":"The last piece is milestones that are both realistic and time-bound . Milestones are how a theme stops being an aspiration and becomes something you can track, \"onboarding completion at 70% by end of Q2,\" not \"make onboarding better, ongoing.\" Can't catch drift \"Make onboarding better ongoing.\" A milestone you can track \"Onboarding completion at 70% by the…"},{"k":"course","t":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Moderate Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: One Product, Several Different Pitches · Lesson 2: Value Propositions That Actually Land · Lesson 3: Building the Launch Plan · Lesson 4: Enabling Sales, Aligning the Rest of the Company · Lesson 5: The Launch Isn't the Finish Line"},{"k":"lesson","t":"One Product, Several Different Pitches","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Moderate Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Launching a product involves more than releasing a feature, it's about connecting with your audience, understanding the landscape, and positioning your product effectively. The first and most crucial step in any go-to-market strategy is defining your audience and positioning your product in a way that resonates with each part of it. Most products are…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Value Propositions That Actually Land","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Moderate Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-moderate&lesson=2","x":"Once you know who's in the room, the next step is saying something that actually matters to each of them. A value proposition needs to communicate how your product solves the specific problem faced by that specific persona, in language they'd actually use themselves. For the end user, that's usually about time and friction: \"Streamline your work so you can…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Building the Launch Plan","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Moderate Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-moderate&lesson=3","x":"Once your target market and messaging are defined, it's time to build the actual launch plan, covering communication, pricing, and how you'll actually reach people. A communication plan outlines how you'll generate awareness: the marketing channels you'll use, a content strategy (case studies, whitepapers, real examples), and social proof like testimonials…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Enabling Sales, Aligning the Rest of the Company","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Moderate Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-moderate&lesson=4","x":"A go-to-market strategy is only as strong as the team executing it, and that extends past marketing into sales enablement and cross-functional alignment. Your sales team needs more than a pitch deck, they need product demos highlighting real differentiators, scripts and FAQs for handling common objections, and regular training to stay current as the product…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Launch Isn't the Finish Line","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Moderate Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-moderate&lesson=5","x":"Launching a product isn't the end, it's the beginning of the part that actually determines whether the strategy worked. Post-launch, track the metrics that matter: customer acquisition cost (what it costs to win a customer), customer lifetime value (what they're worth over time), and something like a Net Promoter Score to gauge whether people would actually…"},{"k":"course","t":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Lots of Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-experienced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: The Roadmap Is Only as Good as Its Communication · Lesson 2: Transparency Builds Trust · Lesson 3: Holding Short-Term and Long-Term Together · Lesson 4: Building Resilience In · Lesson 5: Measure and Iterate"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Roadmap Is Only as Good as Its Communication","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Lots of Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-experienced&lesson=1","x":"A roadmap is only as effective as the communication plan around it. The same underlying roadmap has to be presented differently to different audiences, because they're each trying to answer a different question. Engineers need the detailed view: epics, tasks, dependencies, and timing. Their question is \"what am I building and when?\" Executives need the…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Transparency Builds Trust","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Lots of Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-experienced&lesson=2","x":"Roadmaps change. Features get deprioritized, timelines slip, bets get abandoned. How you communicate those changes determines whether stakeholders keep trusting the roadmap at all. Two practices: Share changes proactively. Public roadmap updates through town halls, an advisory group, or a dedicated channel, so people hear about a shift from you, not through…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Holding Short-Term and Long-Term Together","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Lots of Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-experienced&lesson=3","x":"The hardest part of managing a roadmap is balancing immediate demands, urgent user needs, a competitor move, a compliance deadline, against long-term strategic initiatives that only pay off later. long-term arc quarterly increments deliver now… …each checked against the arc Two mechanisms keep the two in balance: Time-box long-term planning. Dedicated…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Building Resilience In","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Lots of Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-experienced&lesson=4","x":"An experienced roadmap owner assumes the landscape will shift, regulations, competitors, technology, priorities, and builds the roadmap so it can absorb that without being torn up. This is what \"adapting without losing the thread\" looks like in practice."},{"k":"lesson","t":"Measure and Iterate","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Lots of Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-experienced&lesson=5","x":"A roadmap isn't static, it evolves based on feedback, market shifts, and outcomes. To iterate it well, you need to actually measure whether it's working. Roadmap KPIs that tell you something: Time-to-market — how fast a theme moves from conception to release. User satisfaction — the change in satisfaction after a significant roadmap initiative ships.…"},{"k":"course","t":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Advanced Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-advanced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Agents as Decision Participants · Lesson 2: Ground the Agent in Product Reality · Lesson 3: Intentionally Opinionated · Lesson 4: Embed the Agent in Real Product Moments · Lesson 5: Maintain It as a Living System"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Agents as Decision Participants","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Advanced Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-advanced&lesson=1","x":"Large language models are powerful, but without direction they behave like highly articulate generalists. Real leverage comes from intentionally shaping an agent's product judgment so it understands your customers, your constraints, and your definition of success. A product-focused agent is not a chatbot or a feature. It is a decision participant . The most…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Ground the Agent in Product Reality","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Advanced Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-advanced&lesson=2","x":"LLMs have broad knowledge but no inherent understanding of your product. High-performing product agents are grounded in four categories of context, and that context should be persistently available , not re-introduced in every interaction. Product truths Strategic constraints Customer signals Product philosophy the real problem you solve primary and…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Intentionally Opinionated","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Advanced Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-advanced&lesson=3","x":"Generic system prompts produce generic output. High-quality product agents are intentionally opinionated in the same way strong product leaders are. Examples of productive bias: Question feature requests unless they tie to a clear user outcome. Surface second-order effects before recommending solutions. Prioritize long-term customer trust over short-term…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Embed the Agent in Real Product Moments","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Advanced Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-advanced&lesson=4","x":"Agents fail when they exist outside the product lifecycle. They succeed when they're embedded into the moments where judgment matters most. Instead of asking the agent for answers, assign it a role in each moment. Discovery PRDs Roadmap Launch Post- launch Same agent, same skeptic's role, at every stage Assign it a role at each point &#8212; don't wait for…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Maintain It as a Living System","s":"Product Strategy & Roadmapping · Advanced Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=strategy-advanced&lesson=5","x":"Products evolve, and agents must evolve with them. Strong teams refresh agent context quarterly: remove outdated assumptions, add new customer insights, adjust the built-in bias as strategy changes. An outdated agent quietly reinforces old thinking and introduces risk, it's a confident voice arguing from last year's constraints. The real advantage isn't…"},{"k":"framework","t":"Prioritization & Trade-offs","s":"Skill area in the framework","u":"framework.html#prioritization","x":"The discipline of deciding what to work on next, and just as importantly, what not to, given limited time and resources, in a way that holds up under real pressure."},{"k":"course","t":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Limited Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-novice&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: The Loudest-Voice Problem · Lesson 2: A Value-vs-Effort View · Lesson 3: Score, Don't Argue · Lesson 4: Non-Negotiables Come First · Lesson 5: Saying No With a Reason"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Loudest-Voice Problem","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Limited Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-novice&lesson=1","x":"The most common early-stage prioritization pattern is reactive : the next thing worked on is whatever was requested most recently, or by the person who pushed hardest. It feels responsive, and it has three quiet costs. You optimize for advocacy, not value. The best-connected requester wins, which has little to do with what helps users most. You can't defend…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"A Value-vs-Effort View","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Limited Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-novice&lesson=2","x":"The simplest tool for making the comparison explicit is a value-versus-effort view: for each candidate, estimate how much value it creates and how much effort it takes, then plot it. value &#8594; effort &#8594; Quick wins Big bets Fill-ins Money pits inline validation CSV export SSO + audit log theme picker rebuild legacy report Four rough zones fall out…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Score, Don't Argue","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Limited Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-novice&lesson=3","x":"\"Value\" and \"effort\" are still fuzzy. A lightweight scoring rubric breaks them into a few factors you rate quickly, so two people comparing the same item land in roughly the same place. Impact + Reach + Confidence each rated 1&#8211;3 &#247; Effort 1&#8211;3 = score Low confidence drags the score down &#8212; which is the point. Try it yourself below. Slide…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Non-Negotiables Come First","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Limited Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-novice&lesson=4","x":"A scoring rubric is for the things you have a genuine choice about. Some work isn't a choice, and trying to score it just produces a weird-looking ranking. Non-negotiables go straight to the top, unscored: regulatory or legal requirements, security fixes, contractual commitments, anything where not doing it carries a severe, non-linear cost. You're not…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Saying No With a Reason","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Limited Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-novice&lesson=5","x":"The point of all this structure isn't the ranking, it's the ability to say no without damaging the relationship. Reactive prioritization makes every \"no\" feel personal, because there's no principle behind it, just \"we're not doing yours.\" A scored, defensible order changes the conversation: \"here's where your request landed, it scored lower on impact and…"},{"k":"course","t":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Moderate Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Why the Framework Slips · Lesson 2: Put Numbers Behind \"Value\" · Lesson 3: Validate the Estimate · Lesson 4: Same Rubric, Every Time · Lesson 5: Close the Loop"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Why the Framework Slips","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Moderate Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-moderate&lesson=1","x":"At this level you already have a prioritization framework, a value-vs-effort view, maybe a scoring rubric. The problem is it doesn't hold up: the same feature scores \"high value\" in one meeting and \"medium\" in the next, and the rankings feel like they could have gone either way. The framework isn't broken. The inputs are soft. \"Value\" is being estimated by…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Put Numbers Behind \"Value\"","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Moderate Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-moderate&lesson=2","x":"To ground the value estimate, tie every candidate to a specific metric it should move and a rough size of that movement. Shifts with the room \"This improves onboarding.\" &#8594; how much? for whom? says who? Debatable with evidence \"Raises onboarding completion from 58% to ~68%, by cutting two steps before first value.\" Not \"this improves onboarding\" but…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Validate the Estimate","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Moderate Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-moderate&lesson=3","x":"A metric-backed value estimate is still an estimate. Before committing heavily to a high-effort item, check the guess cheaply. estimate metric + size cheap test A/B, prototype, pilot commit drop / rethink A controlled A/B test , a prototype in front of a few users, or a small pilot tells you whether the metric actually moves in the direction and roughly the…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Same Rubric, Every Time","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Moderate Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-moderate&lesson=4","x":"The last source of inconsistency is applying the method differently from one decision to the next, different factors, different weightings, different people's interpretation of \"impact.\" Fixing that is about discipline, not sophistication."},{"k":"lesson","t":"Close the Loop","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Moderate Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-moderate&lesson=5","x":"The thing that makes a prioritization framework trustworthy over time is checking whether it was right. After a prioritized item ships, compare the result against the value estimate you made: did onboarding completion actually go from 58% to 68%, or did it barely move? Compare to the baseline you recorded before, not to a vague memory. A wrong estimate…"},{"k":"course","t":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Lots of Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-experienced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: The Feature Fallacy · Lesson 2: Outcome → Evidence → Minimum · Lesson 3: From Roadmaps to Bet Maps · Lesson 4: Five Moves for the Quarter · Lesson 5: The Courage to Delete"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Feature Fallacy","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Lots of Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-experienced&lesson=1","x":"If you measure progress by how many features ship, you'll eventually ship yourself into irrelevance. The teams that win aren't the ones with the longest release notes, they're the ones with the shortest path to a meaningful outcome. This isn't anti-shipping. It's pro value density : the ratio of outcome created to effort required. When value density goes…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Outcome → Evidence → Minimum","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Lots of Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-experienced&lesson=2","x":"Here's a simple loop that travels well across domains. Think OEM . Outcome observable end state Evidence falsifiable hypothesis Minimum smallest intervention measure the path, then scale or kill Outcome-first. Define the end state in observable terms: \"reduce median prescription time from 4:30 to 2:00.\" Not \"improve the orders experience.\" Evidence-backed.…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"From Roadmaps to Bet Maps","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Lots of Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-experienced&lesson=3","x":"A roadmap says \"we will build these things.\" A bet map says \"we believe doing X for Y segment will drive Z outcome,\" and then holds itself accountable. Each entry on a bet map carries four things a feature list never does: A confidence level. How sure are we, and why? Low confidence isn't a reason not to bet, it's a reason to keep the bet small. Leading…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Five Moves for the Quarter","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Lots of Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-experienced&lesson=4","x":"Concrete ways to raise value density on a real critical path. Step through them."},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Courage to Delete","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Lots of Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-experienced&lesson=5","x":"Value density has a numerator and a denominator. Everything so far has been about the numerator, the outcome. Deletion is how you work the denominator. Create a quarterly decommission list . Remove underused features, consolidate patterns, redesign flows that require explanation. Celebrate deletion with the same fanfare as launches, because a flow that no…"},{"k":"course","t":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Advanced Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-advanced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: From Your Backlog to the System · Lesson 2: A Shared, Legible Method · Lesson 3: Tie It to the P&L · Lesson 4: Portfolio Thinking · Lesson 5: Navigating the Conflict"},{"k":"lesson","t":"From Your Backlog to the System","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Advanced Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-advanced&lesson=1","x":"At earlier levels, prioritization skill is about ordering your own work well. At an advanced level, the deliverable changes: you design prioritization systems that other teams adopt , and you navigate prioritization conflicts at an organizational level , the ones where two teams' top priorities compete for the same capacity or the same customers. The test:…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"A Shared, Legible Method","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Advanced Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-advanced&lesson=2","x":"A prioritization method other teams will actually adopt has three properties. Team A Team B Team C one method shared defs + data ranked comparable Shared criteria and definitions , so \"impact\" and \"value\" mean the same thing on Team A and Team C. A common data source , one place everyone pulls the metrics from, so nobody's arguing from a different number.…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Tie It to the P&L","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Advanced Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-advanced&lesson=3","x":"At the organizational level, the language decisions get made in is financial . An advanced prioritization system connects the ranking to profit-and-loss data so the trade-offs speak that language. Prioritize by expected ROI. Use P&L data to rank initiatives by their potential for revenue growth or cost savings, not just by a felt value score. Link revenue…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Portfolio Thinking","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Advanced Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-advanced&lesson=4","x":"With multiple products or teams, prioritization isn't only within a backlog, it's allocating capacity across them. That's portfolio management. Total product + engineering capacity Product A &#183; 45% growth Product B &#183; 28% mature C &#183; 27% growth Set the cross-product split first &#8212; then each team prioritizes within its slice. Skip this step…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Navigating the Conflict","s":"Prioritization & Trade-offs · Advanced Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=prioritization-advanced&lesson=5","x":"The moment the whole system exists to handle: two teams' top priorities collide, competing for the same capacity, the same customers, or the same launch window. Without a shared method, this resolves by escalation, whoever gets to the executive first, or argues most forcefully, wins. With one, the conversation changes: both items are scored against the same…"},{"k":"framework","t":"Execution & Delivery","s":"Skill area in the framework","u":"framework.html#execution","x":"Turning decisions into shipped, working product, reliably and without unnecessary drama, from an idea all the way through launch and the iteration that follows."},{"k":"course","t":"Execution & Delivery · Limited Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-novice&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Why Surprises Show Up Late · Lesson 2: Work in Increments, Get Feedback Early · Lesson 3: Automate the Checks · Lesson 4: Track a Few Quality Numbers · Lesson 5: Predictability Is the Goal"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Why Surprises Show Up Late","s":"Execution & Delivery · Limited Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-novice&lesson=1","x":"Unpredictable timelines and end-of-project surprises usually aren't a discipline problem. They're a structure problem. When a piece of work is built as one large batch and only integrated and tested near the end, the first honest signal about whether it works, whether it fits together, whether the estimate held, arrives when there's no time left to react.…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Work in Increments, Get Feedback Early","s":"Execution & Delivery · Limited Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-novice&lesson=2","x":"The core practice is delivering value incrementally and iteratively instead of all at once. BIG BATCH build build build first test, no time left INCREMENTS tested every step Three habits make this concrete: define a small MVP scope , the essential features that address the core need without overextending; adopt an agile cadence (Scrum or Kanban) so you…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Automate the Checks","s":"Execution & Delivery · Limited Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-novice&lesson=3","x":"Increments only give you early signal if something is actually checking each one. Manual testing at the end of a project is exactly the late-signal problem again. commit automated tests CI/CD build deploy a break stops here, in isolation &#8212; the day it's introduced Automated tests , regression, functional, and security, run on every change, so a break…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Track a Few Quality Numbers","s":"Execution & Delivery · Limited Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-novice&lesson=4","x":"You can't tell whether execution is actually improving without measuring it. Pick a small set of quality metrics and watch them. Here's the same defect-rate metric under the two structures from this course, drag the slider across six releases:"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Predictability Is the Goal","s":"Execution & Delivery · Limited Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-novice&lesson=5","x":"Put the pieces together and the picture is simple: small scope , an agile cadence , automated checks on every change, and a few quality metrics reviewed regularly. Each one moves the moment you learn about a problem earlier. That's the whole difference between execution that \"happens\" with unpredictable timelines and execution you can forecast. You're not…"},{"k":"course","t":"Execution & Delivery · Moderate Experience","s":"6 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Accessibility as Innovation, Not Obligation · Lesson 2: Where AI Is Already Changing Accessibility · Lesson 3: AI Is an Enabler, Not a Shortcut · Lesson 4: The Rollout Playbook · Lesson 5: Measure What Actually Matters · Lesson 6: Quick Wins You Can Turn On Today"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Accessibility as Innovation, Not Obligation","s":"Execution & Delivery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-moderate&lesson=1","x":"For years, digital accessibility was treated as a compliance checkbox or a technical afterthought. AI is helping shift that mindset, creating experiences that are more inclusive, more adaptive, and more human-centered from the start. This isn't a niche feature set. When products are designed to meet people where they are, they become easier to use for…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Where AI Is Already Changing Accessibility","s":"Execution & Delivery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-moderate&lesson=2","x":"Four categories cover most of what's changing right now. Voice interfaces let people with mobility or dexterity challenges complete tasks through natural conversation instead of typing or tapping. Visual assistance turns a smartphone camera into a real-time guide for people who are blind or have low vision, identifying objects, reading text aloud,…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"AI Is an Enabler, Not a Shortcut","s":"Execution & Delivery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-moderate&lesson=3","x":"AI is not a perfect solution on its own. Voice systems can still struggle with accents or speech patterns. Image recognition can miss context. Captions can introduce errors. Accessibility cannot rely entirely on automation. Inclusive products require testing with real users, thoughtful design decisions, and a commitment to improving outcomes over time.…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Rollout Playbook","s":"Execution & Delivery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-moderate&lesson=4","x":"For teams wondering how to get started, the best approach begins with real user needs, not the technology itself. Step through the six-stage path from there. 1 &#183; Find friction 2 &#183; Pick 1&#8211;2 cases 3 &#183; Right people 4 &#183; Success metrics 5 &#183; Pilot & refine 6 &#183; Expand Start from user friction, not the technology &#8212; and only…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Measure What Actually Matters","s":"Execution & Delivery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-moderate&lesson=5","x":"Define what success looks like before you build: lower task abandonment, faster completion time, better comprehension, higher satisfaction, or improved usability for specific groups. AI accessibility features should be evaluated not only by technical performance, but by whether they genuinely reduce friction and increase independence. Transparency matters…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Quick Wins You Can Turn On Today","s":"Execution & Delivery · Moderate Experience · Lesson 6","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-moderate&lesson=6","x":"Some of the most useful accessibility improvements don't require a long implementation plan at all, you can often just turn them on. Enable live captions by default for meetings and webinars. Turn on meeting transcripts so details are searchable later. Use AI recaps after customer calls so teams stay aligned without repeating information. Add automatic alt…"},{"k":"course","t":"Execution & Delivery · Lots of Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-experienced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Contingency vs. Hope · Lesson 2: Design for Failure · Lesson 3: Rehearse the Peak · Lesson 4: Build Checkpoints Into the Process · Lesson 5: A Framework for Change"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Contingency vs. Hope","s":"Execution & Delivery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-experienced&lesson=1","x":"At an experienced level, the job is delivering complex, cross-team initiatives reliably . The thing that makes delivery reliable isn't better luck, it's building in contingency for the unexpected rather than hoping nothing goes wrong. \"Hope\" as an execution strategy looks like: a single path with no fallback, monitoring that tells you a system is down after…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Design for Failure","s":"Execution & Delivery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-experienced&lesson=2","x":"A reliable system assumes its own components will fail and keeps working anyway. traffic balancer node A node B node C down still up The building blocks: redundancy and load balancing so one failed component doesn't take the product down; proactive monitoring and alerting so you detect a degradation before users report it; and an incident response plan…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Rehearse the Peak","s":"Execution & Delivery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-experienced&lesson=3","x":"The failures that hurt most are the ones that only appear under load, a launch spike, a seasonal surge, a big new customer going live. Hoping the system holds is not a plan; rehearsing is. Performance testing and load simulation mean deliberately pushing the system to and past its expected peak in a controlled setting, so you find the bottleneck, the query…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Build Checkpoints Into the Process","s":"Execution & Delivery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-experienced&lesson=4","x":"The other source of late crises is a constraint discovered late, a security requirement, a legal review, a compliance rule that should have shaped the design and instead forces a rebuild. The fix is structural: put the checkpoints in the process, not at the end of it."},{"k":"lesson","t":"A Framework for Change","s":"Execution & Delivery · Lots of Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-experienced&lesson=5","x":"The last unexpected thing to plan for is the requirements changing mid-flight, a new regulation, a platform policy shift, a contractual obligation. Experienced teams handle this with a standing change management framework rather than treating each change as a one-off fire. The framework defines, in advance: Who assesses a change — the named people (product,…"},{"k":"course","t":"Execution & Delivery · Advanced Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-advanced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: From Running Launches to Building the Machine · Lesson 2: Delivery Rhythms Worth Copying · Lesson 3: Coaching Through a High-Stakes Launch · Lesson 4: Data-Driven Optimization as Standard Practice · Lesson 5: Make It Outlast You"},{"k":"lesson","t":"From Running Launches to Building the Machine","s":"Execution & Delivery · Advanced Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-advanced&lesson=1","x":"At an experienced level, you deliver complex work reliably yourself. At an advanced level, the deliverable changes: you design delivery systems and rhythms that other teams adopt , and you coach other people through complex, high-stakes launches . The test: if two other teams in the organization are running the delivery cadence you designed, without you in…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Delivery Rhythms Worth Copying","s":"Execution & Delivery · Advanced Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-advanced&lesson=2","x":"A delivery system other teams adopt is a small set of rhythms and defaults, documented well enough to lift wholesale. plan increment build + auto-test ship via CI/CD read the metrics The pieces worth standardizing: an iterative roadmap format that flexes between fixes, enhancements, and new work; CI/CD and automated testing as the default , not a per-team…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Coaching Through a High-Stakes Launch","s":"Execution & Delivery · Advanced Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-advanced&lesson=3","x":"The other half of the advanced role is walking a less experienced team through a launch that matters, in a way that builds their capability rather than just getting this one out the door."},{"k":"lesson","t":"Data-Driven Optimization as Standard Practice","s":"Execution & Delivery · Advanced Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-advanced&lesson=4","x":"At earlier levels, \"look at the data after a release\" is a good habit. At an advanced level, it's a standard practice every team runs by default, not a special project someone champions. That means, as a normal part of the delivery loop: Analyze user behaviour after each meaningful release, feature adoption, engagement, where people drop off. Track whether…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Make It Outlast You","s":"Execution & Delivery · Advanced Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=execution-advanced&lesson=5","x":"A delivery system is only advanced if it survives you leaving. The last job is durability. A continuous-improvement culture. Teams regularly analyze what shipped, refine the process, and are recognized for incremental gains, not just big launches. The system improves itself. Knowledge sharing built in. Delivery playbooks, launch retros shared org-wide,…"},{"k":"framework","t":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence","s":"Skill area in the framework","u":"framework.html#stakeholder","x":"Building genuine alignment and trust across the many different people, teams, and functions a product touches, especially the ones that don't report to you."},{"k":"course","t":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Limited Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-novice&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Stop Waiting to Be Asked · Lesson 2: Build a Rhythm · Lesson 3: Set Up the Channels · Lesson 4: Tailor the Update · Lesson 5: Transparency Is the Habit"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Stop Waiting to Be Asked","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Limited Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-novice&lesson=1","x":"The most common early-career communication pattern is reactive : you share information when someone asks for it. It feels responsive, but it has two quiet failures. People who don't know to ask stay uninformed. And every \"quick question\" is an interruption that could have been prevented by an update nobody had to request. Proactive communication flips that:…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Build a Rhythm","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Limited Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-novice&lesson=2","x":"Proactive communication runs on cadence , a set of recurring touchpoints so predictable that people stop needing to ask. Daily Weekly Per sprint Quarterly standup team sync review + retro roadmap review The exact structure matters less than the predictability. Regular standups keep the team aligned day to day. A weekly sync aligns on deliverables and…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Set Up the Channels","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Limited Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-novice&lesson=3","x":"A cadence needs somewhere for the information to live between meetings. Three basics: Shared board Open channel Collaborative docs Default state: informed Staying uninformed should take effort, not the other way around. A shared project tool (Jira, Linear, Trello, whatever your team uses) where progress is visible without anyone reporting it. If a…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Tailor the Update","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Limited Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-novice&lesson=4","x":"Broadcasting the same update to everyone is better than staying silent, but it still isn't quite right. Different audiences need different cuts of the same information."},{"k":"lesson","t":"Transparency Is the Habit","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Limited Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-novice&lesson=5","x":"The through-line of everything so far is transparency by default : sharing status, decisions, setbacks, and trade-offs before you're asked, in a place people can see, at an altitude that fits the audience. The part that's easy to skip is the setbacks. It's tempting to only broadcast wins and stay quiet when something slips. But a stakeholder who only hears…"},{"k":"course","t":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Moderate Experience","s":"4 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: The Roles on a Product Team, and Why Each One Matters · Lesson 2: Collaboration Doesn't Happen by Accident · Lesson 3: Empowerment, Not Just Direction · Lesson 4: Recognition and Continuous Learning"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Roles on a Product Team, and Why Each One Matters","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Moderate Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Creating an impactful product isn't just about the product itself, it's about the team behind it. A well-rounded product team requires a diverse set of skills and clearly understood roles. Step through the core roles you'll typically find on a strong product team. What each role needs from you (the PM) Product Owner UX / UI Design Engineering Quality…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Collaboration Doesn't Happen by Accident","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Moderate Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-moderate&lesson=2","x":"Building a high-performing product team isn't just about assigning the right roles, it's about fostering effective collaboration between them. Agile methodologies like Scrum or Kanban provide the structure needed for iterative development and cross-functional alignment: regular sprint planning, stand-ups, and retrospectives, plus a shared tool for…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Empowerment, Not Just Direction","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Moderate Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-moderate&lesson=3","x":"High-performing teams need more than the right skills, they need motivation, autonomy, and support. As a product leader, your role is to provide clear direction while cultivating a culture of ownership. Start with clarity: use a framework like OKRs (Objectives and Key Results) to define quarterly objectives and the metrics that track progress against them.…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Recognition and Continuous Learning","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Moderate Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-moderate&lesson=4","x":"Celebrate wins, both big and small, and recognize team members who go above and beyond. This isn't just a nice gesture, it's what fosters a positive team culture and keeps people motivated over the long run: publicly acknowledging contributions in meetings, offering real incentives for exceptional work, and encouraging peer-to-peer recognition. The work…"},{"k":"course","t":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Lots of Experience","s":"4 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-experienced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: A Shared Vision Is the Starting Point · Lesson 2: Working Well With Engineering and Design · Lesson 3: Bridging Product and Risk, Legal, or Compliance · Lesson 4: Building a Customer-Centric Culture Across Teams"},{"k":"lesson","t":"A Shared Vision Is the Starting Point","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Lots of Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-experienced&lesson=1","x":"Success in product work hinges on collective collaboration between engineering, design, marketing, sales, compliance, and support. That collaboration isn't automatic, it requires an intentional approach to align teams toward a shared mission, especially given how different each function's priorities and incentives naturally are. A shared vision acts as the…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Working Well With Engineering and Design","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Lots of Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-experienced&lesson=2","x":"Maintaining strong relationships with engineering and design is critical to turning vision into reality, and each requires a different kind of care. With engineering, act as a liaison between user needs and technical capability. Respect the real technical constraints your engineers are navigating, especially around sensitive data or complex integrations,…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Bridging Product and Risk, Legal, or Compliance","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Lots of Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-experienced&lesson=3","x":"In any regulated or risk-sensitive space, product managers have to navigate a complex landscape of rules and standards, and that means collaborating closely with legal, security, or compliance functions rather than treating them as a late-stage checkpoint. Engage these functions early in the development cycle. Waiting until a feature is nearly finished to…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Building a Customer-Centric Culture Across Teams","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Lots of Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-experienced&lesson=4","x":"Product success doesn't depend only on internal teams working well together, it also depends on building a genuinely customer-centric culture that continuously incorporates real feedback. Establish real feedback loops: customer advisory groups with key users, and user testing or beta programs that validate new features before a full rollout. Collecting…"},{"k":"course","t":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Advanced Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-advanced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: From Managing Relationships to Shaping the System · Lesson 2: Building an Aligned Cross-Functional Culture · Lesson 3: Product, Marketing, and Sales as One Motion · Lesson 4: Mentoring Stakeholder Skill · Lesson 5: Making It Stick"},{"k":"lesson","t":"From Managing Relationships to Shaping the System","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Advanced Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-advanced&lesson=1","x":"At earlier levels, stakeholder skill is personal: you build trust with engineering, you keep your sponsor informed, you navigate a disagreement well. At an advanced level, the job changes. You're now responsible for how the organization communicates and collaborates cross-functionally , as a system, and for mentoring other people to operate it. The tell…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Building an Aligned Cross-Functional Culture","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Advanced Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-advanced&lesson=2","x":"Aligning an organization is deliberate work with a few load-bearing pieces. Product shared vision Eng Design Sales Mktg Support Risk A shared vision , set with key stakeholders involved early, so every function can see why the product exists and what it's for. Org-wide communication channels — regular cross-functional syncs and a genuine single source of…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Product, Marketing, and Sales as One Motion","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Advanced Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-advanced&lesson=3","x":"Advanced cross-functional work means the go-to-market functions operate as one motion with product, not a handoff. Build buyer personas with marketing , from shared user research and segmentation, so the people you're designing for and the people they're messaging to are the same people. Align messaging to real value propositions. Marketing's claims and the…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Mentoring Stakeholder Skill","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Advanced Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-advanced&lesson=4","x":"A system that only you can run isn't finished. The advanced level includes deliberately growing stakeholder skill in the people around you."},{"k":"lesson","t":"Making It Stick","s":"Stakeholder Communication & Influence · Advanced Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=stakeholder-advanced&lesson=5","x":"The last job is making the collaboration system durable, and knowing whether it's actually working. Turn feedback loops into standing institutions. Customer advisory groups, beta programs, and cross-functional reviews should be permanent fixtures with owners, not things that happen when someone remembers. Close loops org-wide. When recurring feedback themes…"},{"k":"framework","t":"AI Fluency — Individual","s":"Skill area in the framework","u":"framework.html#aiIndividual","x":"How effectively someone personally uses AI tools to remove friction from their own work, and how well they've developed judgment about where to trust AI output and where to double-check it."},{"k":"course","t":"AI Fluency — Individual · Limited Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-novice&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Nobody Is Ready, and That's Okay · Lesson 2: The Shape of the Journey · Lesson 3: How to Actually Get Started · Lesson 4: How to Scale Once You Have Traction · Lesson 5: The Whole Playbook"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Nobody Is Ready, and That's Okay","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Limited Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-novice&lesson=1","x":"The same conversation happens constantly: someone brings up AI, heads nod, and then someone says, \"We're just not ready for that yet.\" Here's the thing worth sitting with: waiting for \"ready\" is the same as deciding not to go. Nobody is ready. That's okay. You start anyway. The biggest misconception is that adopting AI means building something, buying an…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Shape of the Journey","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Limited Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-novice&lesson=2","x":"Before getting into the specific steps, it helps to see the whole shape of where this is going. Adoption doesn't jump straight from \"nobody's using this\" to \"the whole org runs on it.\" It moves through stages, and each one builds the evidence for the next. Notice where it starts: with you , not a company initiative. Everything else on this path is built on…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"How to Actually Get Started","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Limited Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-novice&lesson=3","x":"Four concrete moves to make, step by step. None of them require permission from anyone but you, until the fourth one."},{"k":"lesson","t":"How to Scale Once You Have Traction","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Limited Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-novice&lesson=4","x":"Once you have a few small wins, scaling gets a lot less scary, you're building on evidence instead of theory. Three things that actually move the needle: One person's win Documented as a template Others pick it up Org capability The template is the bridge &#8212; without it the win stays stuck with one person. Share what's working. A Slack thread, a monthly…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Whole Playbook","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Limited Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-novice&lesson=5","x":"Here's the bottom line: you don't need a perfect strategy. You don't need executive buy-in on day one. You don't need to understand the technology inside and out before you try it. You need one use case, one experiment, and one person willing to share what they learned. Everything else grows from there. Start small. Stay curious. Share what you find. That's…"},{"k":"course","t":"AI Fluency — Individual · Moderate Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: From Tasks to Rituals · Lesson 2: Backlog Grooming, Before and After · Lesson 3: Research and Experiments as Always-On Engines · Lesson 4: KPI Reviews — From Reporting Meeting to Decision Meeting · Lesson 5: The PM as Conductor"},{"k":"lesson","t":"From Tasks to Rituals","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Moderate Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Most product managers didn't sign up to be professional plate spinners, yet the days fill up with writing PRDs, chasing status, pulling data for reviews, and answering the same question in slightly different formats. The strategic work gets squeezed into the margins. The mindset shift that changes this: stop asking \"what can I ask an AI model for?\" and…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Backlog Grooming, Before and After","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Moderate Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-moderate&lesson=2","x":"Grooming is the classic ritual to hand a first pass to an agent. BEFORE AFTER Collect tickets, Slack, requests De-duplicate and clarify by hand Rewrite titles for engineering Re-prioritize on gut feel slow, noisy, inconsistent Agent clusters incoming items by theme Agent flags duplicates Agent drafts problem statements Agent proposes priority from your…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Research and Experiments as Always-On Engines","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Moderate Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-moderate&lesson=3","x":"Two more rituals follow the same shape: the agent runs continuously in the background, you turn its output into decisions. Market research. Instead of scrambling when a competitor appears or leadership asks about a space, an \"Insights\" agent tracks competitor release notes, pricing pages, job postings, and analyst reports, and produces a weekly digest. Your…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"KPI Reviews — From Reporting Meeting to Decision Meeting","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Moderate Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-moderate&lesson=4","x":"Most PMs have too many dashboards and too little insight. KPI reviews turn into a meeting where everyone reads numbers out loud. A \"Metrics\" agent monitors your core metrics, alerts you to anomalies, writes a weekly or monthly \"state of the product\" summary, highlights cohorts or flows that changed meaningfully, and suggests follow-up questions and possible…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The PM as Conductor","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Moderate Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-moderate&lesson=5","x":"When these rituals have agents built into them, the job itself starts to look different: Less time creating artifacts, more time aligning people. Less energy chasing status, more energy shaping strategy. Fewer one-off tasks, more reusable systems and playbooks. Your calendar shifts toward regular review rituals with AI-prepared inputs, deep-dive sessions…"},{"k":"course","t":"AI Fluency — Individual · Lots of Experience","s":"6 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-experienced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: AI Earned a Seat, Not the Head of the Table · Lesson 2: Start With Human-Led Conversations · Lesson 3: Use Agents to Synthesize, Not Interpret · Lesson 4: Let Humans Do the Sense-Making · Lesson 5: Use AI to Generate and Challenge Hypotheses · Lesson 6: Close the Loop, and the Most Common Failure Mode"},{"k":"lesson","t":"AI Earned a Seat, Not the Head of the Table","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Lots of Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-experienced&lesson=1","x":"AI has earned a real seat at the product discovery table. The problem is that many teams are letting it sit at the head. Discovery is faster now, interviews get summarized automatically, feedback gets analyzed at scale. All of that is true, and yet many teams are making worse decisions than before. The issue isn't AI itself. It's where teams place it in the…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Start With Human-Led Conversations","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Lots of Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-experienced&lesson=2","x":"No AI workflow can compensate for skipping real conversations. Product discovery still begins the way it always has: someone on the product team talks directly to a customer and listens without trying to sell, validate, or steer toward a predetermined outcome. Customers rarely describe their problems cleanly. They circle around them, contradict themselves,…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Use Agents to Synthesize, Not Interpret","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Lots of Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-experienced&lesson=3","x":"Once you have real conversations, this is where agents earn their keep. Most teams drown in qualitative data, interview notes, support tickets, sales call notes scattered across half a dozen tools. AI agents are excellent at organizing that chaos: grouping feedback by recurring themes, surfacing common phrases, spotting patterns across more data than any…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Let Humans Do the Sense-Making","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Lots of Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-experienced&lesson=4","x":"Synthesis is not the same thing as understanding, and this is where many teams blur the line. Once feedback has been organized, the hard work begins. Deciding what matters isn't a data problem, it's a judgment problem, one that requires context about company strategy, market dynamics, technical constraints, and long-term vision. Humans are responsible for…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Use AI to Generate and Challenge Hypotheses","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Lots of Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-experienced&lesson=5","x":"This is one of the most underutilized, high-leverage uses of AI in discovery. Instead of asking agents what to build, ask them to help you think better. Step through how that actually works."},{"k":"lesson","t":"Close the Loop, and the Most Common Failure Mode","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Lots of Experience · Lesson 6","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-experienced&lesson=6","x":"Discovery doesn't end when insights get written down. It ends when understanding is validated through further conversation. Take your hypotheses back to customers, share prototypes or problem statements, and watch how customers react, not just what they say. Watch for shifts in language. Listen for corrections. Those moments are often more informative than…"},{"k":"course","t":"AI Fluency — Individual · Advanced Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-advanced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: The Shift Nobody Announced · Lesson 2: Morning — Agents Doing the Watching · Lesson 3: Midday — Catching What Would Have Slipped Through · Lesson 4: Afternoon — Editing, Not Authoring · Lesson 5: What's Actually Different"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Shift Nobody Announced","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Advanced Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-advanced&lesson=1","x":"Jordan's alarm goes off at 6:45. By the time coffee is brewing, the first decision of the day has already been made without them, and that's exactly the point of this lesson. This isn't a story about AI replacing a product manager. It's a story about what happens to a PM's day once a handful of specialized agents take over the watching : reading every…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Morning — Agents Doing the Watching","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Advanced Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-advanced&lesson=2","x":"Five years ago, opening an inbox with 62 new emails meant forty minutes of scrolling before the real workday even started. Step through what Jordan's morning looks like instead."},{"k":"lesson","t":"Midday — Catching What Would Have Slipped Through","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Advanced Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-advanced&lesson=3","x":"The most valuable thing an agent can do isn't answering questions, it's noticing something a person wasn't even looking for. Before an 11:00 AM renewal call, an account-context agent pulls together usage trends, support tickets, and repeat feature requests, then flags something easy to miss: usage of a core feature dropped sharply right after last month's…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Afternoon — Editing, Not Authoring","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Advanced Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-advanced&lesson=4","x":"At 2:30, Jordan talks through a rough PRD idea with a writing agent and gets back a first draft: problem statement, proposed approach, open questions. It's not right, the framing is too narrow, focused on the mechanics of the drop-off rather than the real user confusion behind it. Jordan rewrites the problem statement almost entirely, keeps most of the…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"What's Actually Different","s":"AI Fluency — Individual · Advanced Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=aiIndividual-advanced&lesson=5","x":"By 5:45, an end-of-day digest arrives before Jordan even closes the laptop: what happened, what's still open, three things flagged for tomorrow, and an honest note about what it couldn't confirm, since one conversation from standup happened outside any tool it can see. None of this is about agents making decisions instead of Jordan. Every meaningful call…"},{"k":"framework","t":"AI Fluency — Organizational","s":"Skill area in the framework","u":"framework.html#aiOrg","x":"The ability to bring real AI capability into an organization in a way that's durable and governed, and that survives beyond any one enthusiastic individual who happens to be good at it."},{"k":"course","t":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Limited Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-novice&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Why One Chatbot Fails at Real Product Work · Lesson 2: Design Agents Like Roles on a Team · Lesson 3: Start With Workflows, Not Models · Lesson 4: Scope a Minimum Squad, Then Prioritize It · Lesson 5: Plug It In, and Evaluate It Like a Product"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Why One Chatbot Fails at Real Product Work","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Limited Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-novice&lesson=1","x":"Most teams start their AI journey by adding a chatbot. Natural first step, common trap: treating AI as a single general-purpose assistant you ask to do everything. One assistant gets asked to research a market, summarize feedback, propose features, draft PRDs, write launch messaging, analyze experiments, and answer stakeholder questions. Each of those needs…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Design Agents Like Roles on a Team","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Limited Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-novice&lesson=2","x":"Instead of \"an AI,\" think \"a squad.\" Each agent has a role description, a clear scope and boundaries, specific inputs it can use, outputs it's expected to produce, and evaluation metrics tied to that role. A practical starting squad for product teams: Researcher themes + evidence Analyst impact + segments Experiment Designer how to test it Copywriter the…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Start With Workflows, Not Models","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Limited Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-novice&lesson=3","x":"The biggest mistake is starting with the technology. Start with a workflow you want to improve, a recurring product ritual that's expensive in time and attention: weekly backlog grooming, monthly insights review, quarterly planning, experiment review and writeups. Then ask three questions about it: where do we lose time, where do mistakes happen, and where…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Scope a Minimum Squad, Then Prioritize It","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Limited Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-novice&lesson=4","x":"You don't need to automate everything. Design an MVP squad that's useful weekly, small enough to maintain, and easy to evaluate. For example: a Researcher that produces a weekly top-5 customer pain points with source links, an Analyst that writes a weekly KPI narrative for 3 to 5 core metrics, an Experiment Designer that fills in a one-page brief template,…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Plug It In, and Evaluate It Like a Product","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Limited Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-novice&lesson=5","x":"The squad should fit the tools and rituals your team already uses, Slack or Teams for digests, Jira or Linear for turning insights into tickets, Notion or Google Docs for living briefs, your analytics platform for KPI monitoring. The pattern: agents produce outputs on a cadence, humans review in a short ritual, approved outputs become artifacts and actions…"},{"k":"course","t":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Moderate Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Where Your Organization Actually Stands · Lesson 2: Contained Growth vs. Compounding Growth · Lesson 3: The Governance and Change-Management Case · Lesson 4: A Practical Five-Step Path · Lesson 5: The Bigger Picture"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Where Your Organization Actually Stands","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Moderate Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-moderate&lesson=1","x":"Most organizations sit in one of three stages. In the first, novice , AI adoption hasn't really started yet, it's just breaking the surface. In the second, individual enablement , a handful of curious employees have taught themselves to use AI tools well: clever prompts, real time saved, but contained to that one person. This second stage is where the…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Contained Growth vs. Compounding Growth","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Moderate Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-moderate&lesson=2","x":"When you build an AI habit or workflow for one person, you're optimizing that person's time. That's real value, but it's contained, isolated , like a plant in a pot. It scales only as far as their workload and only as long as they keep using it. When you build for a process instead, the value becomes connected and compounding . Drag the slider below to see…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Governance and Change-Management Case","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Moderate Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-moderate&lesson=3","x":"There's a governance angle that's easy to miss. Individual AI use is invisible to the organization, nobody knows what prompts are being run or what decisions are being informed by an ungoverned tool. Organizational tools live inside managed systems, where they can be monitored, versioned, and improved like any other piece of software. There's also a…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"A Practical Five-Step Path","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Moderate Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-moderate&lesson=4","x":"You don't need a full transformation program to make this shift. You need one real process, one working tool, and proof that it works. Step through the five steps below."},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Bigger Picture","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Moderate Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-moderate&lesson=5","x":"Treat your first success as a proof point, not the finish line. The real payoff isn't the single automation, it's what that first win teaches your organization about what's possible, and the credibility it builds for the next one. The organizations that separate themselves over the next few years won't be the ones with the most individual AI enthusiasts.…"},{"k":"course","t":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Lots of Experience","s":"6 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-experienced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Product Rigor, Not IT Rigor · Lesson 2: Discovery — Find the Work Worth Automating · Lesson 3: Pilot — Small, Real, and Closely Watched · Lesson 4: Feedback — Listening for Corrections, Not Compliments · Lesson 5: Iteration — Earn the Next Stage of Rollout · Lesson 6: Adoption — Measure It Like the Product It Is"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Product Rigor, Not IT Rigor","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Lots of Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-experienced&lesson=1","x":"Most agentic AI conversations point outward: agents for customers, AI features to compete on. But some of the highest-leverage AI work never touches a customer at all, it shows up inside the company, changing how sales reps prep for calls or how support triages tickets. Call these PM Copilots . Here's the trap: building a copilot for your own team is still…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Discovery — Find the Work Worth Automating","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Lots of Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-experienced&lesson=2","x":"This is the first of five stages you'll walk through in this course. The instinct with agentic AI is to reach for the most ambitious use case, a copilot that handles an entire renewal end to end sounds impressive in a slide. It's also a terrible place to start. Good discovery here looks like good discovery anywhere else: sit in on real calls, read a week's…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Pilot — Small, Real, and Closely Watched","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Lots of Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-experienced&lesson=3","x":"Once you've picked a use case, resist the urge to roll it out broadly. A pilot with five to ten people doing real work is worth more than a company-wide launch with a flashy announcement. Pick people who represent a range of comfort with new tools, not just your most enthusiastic early adopters. Give them real tasks, not sandboxed test cases, people behave…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Feedback — Listening for Corrections, Not Compliments","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Lots of Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-experienced&lesson=4","x":"Feedback on an internal AI tool rarely shows up as a formal ticket. It shows up as someone quietly editing every draft the copilot produces, or going back to doing the task by hand because it's faster than fixing what the agent got wrong. If you're only listening for complaints in a feedback channel, you'll miss almost everything useful. Step through the…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Iteration — Earn the Next Stage of Rollout","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Lots of Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-experienced&lesson=5","x":"Every round of pilot feedback should change something concrete: adjusting the prompt or guardrails, changing what the agent can do without a human checking its work, or narrowing scope because you tried to solve too much at once. Current version Same corrections every week? Iterate: prompt / guardrails / scope Expand the rollout yes no Here's the rule worth…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Adoption — Measure It Like the Product It Is","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Lots of Experience · Lesson 6","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-experienced&lesson=6","x":"This is the stage most internal AI efforts skip entirely, and it's the one that determines whether the tool survives past its first quarter. If you can't point to numbers showing the copilot is actually helping, it gets quietly deprioritized the moment budget gets tight. Weekly active users among the eligible group tells you whether the tool has staying…"},{"k":"course","t":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Advanced Experience","s":"5 lessons","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-advanced&lesson=1","x":"Lesson 1: Beyond Automation · Lesson 2: What AI Does Well, What Only People Do · Lesson 3: Engineering the Quick Win · Lesson 4: Going the Extra Mile · Lesson 5: The Direction Is Set by Strategy"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Beyond Automation","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Advanced Experience · Lesson 1","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-advanced&lesson=1","x":"The business world is buzzing about AI as the ultimate driver of efficiency, personalization, and growth. That's true, and many organizations are still missing the point. In the race to automate, they risk sacrificing the one thing that builds lasting loyalty: the human factor. Technology on its own is cold. It can be efficient without being empathetic, and…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"What AI Does Well, What Only People Do","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Advanced Experience · Lesson 2","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-advanced&lesson=2","x":"Blending the two starts with being honest about each one's strengths. AI handles the \"what\" People deliver the \"how\" 24/7 availability on common issues Data-driven personalization Routing, summarizing history Scaled, instant, consistent efficient, not empathetic Empathy for charged moments Creative, off-script problem-solving Bending rules when it's right…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Engineering the Quick Win","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Advanced Experience · Lesson 3","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-advanced&lesson=3","x":"The forward-thinking model isn't AI or people, it's collaborative intelligence : AI as a co-pilot that identifies the perfect moment for a human to step in and deliver a \"quick win\" that builds loyalty. Proactive, personal outreach. AI flags an account that keeps failing to use a new feature correctly. Instead of firing off a generic help article, it alerts…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"Going the Extra Mile","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Advanced Experience · Lesson 4","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-advanced&lesson=4","x":"Beyond quick wins, clearing volume off your team frees the most valuable resource of all: time to genuinely understand a customer's world. A customer in a specialized field, say renewable-energy logistics, is struggling to adapt a standard project-management tool to their needs. An AI logs the ticket. A human agent could hand over a standard workaround and…"},{"k":"lesson","t":"The Direction Is Set by Strategy","s":"AI Fluency — Organizational · Advanced Experience · Lesson 5","u":"lesson.html?course=aiOrg-advanced&lesson=5","x":"AI is a powerful force, but its direction is guided by your strategy. Two organizations can deploy the same capability and get opposite results, one hollows out its customer relationships, the other deepens them. The advanced organizational skill is making that direction explicit and consistent across teams: defining where AI absorbs volume, where humans…"}];
